course: books
lesson: 27
title: The Wind in the Willows, chapter I, part 3: The River Bank (the Otter, a glimpse of Badger, and Toad in his new boat)
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: While they eat, the Mole notices a line of bubbles moving along the water. The Rat chirrups, and up climbs the Otter, shaking himself and grumbling «Greedy beggars! Why didn't you invite me?» He and the Mole are friends at once.
explain: A stripy head peeps out of a hedge: Badger. «H'm! Company,» he grunts, and turns his back and disappears. That's just like him, says the Rat — he hates crowds and parties.
explain: Toad is out on the river, says the Otter, in a brand-new racing boat with new clothes and new everything. The Rat and the Otter laugh: Toad is always mad about something new — sailing, then punting, then a house-boat — and always gets bored of it. They watch him splash past, rolling badly but rowing his hardest.
explain: The Otter starts a funny story about Toad — and stops mid-sentence: a May-fly wobbles past, there's a swirl and a «cloop!», and both the May-fly and the Otter are gone. The Mole remembers that good animals never comment when a friend vanishes suddenly.
explain: The Mole asks to pack the basket too. It's not as fun as unpacking — he finds a forgotten plate, then the Rat points out a fork, and last of all he finds he's been sitting on the mustard pot — but he gets it done without losing his temper.
fact: The Mole sees «a streak of bubbles that I see travelling along the surface of the water»; the Rat says «Bubbles? Oho!» because he knows who makes them.
fact: «A broad glistening muzzle showed itself above the edge of the bank, and the Otter hauled himself out». His first words: «Greedy beggars! Why didn't you invite me, Ratty?»
fact: The Rat: «This was an impromptu affair» (impromptu = not planned, made up on the spot). The Otter: «Proud, I'm sure» — an old polite way of saying «pleased to meet you».
fact: The Otter says he came to the backwater for «a moment's peace» and then says sorry because that sounds as if he didn't want to meet them — he puts his foot in it, then tries to take it back.
fact: Badger: «a stripy head, with high shoulders behind it» peered out of a hedge, grunted «H'm! Company,» and «turned his back and disappeared from view».
fact: The Rat: «Simply hates Society!» — Society here means parties, visitors and polite company.
fact: «Toad's out, for one ... In his brand-new wager-boat; new togs, new everything!» A wager-boat is a light, narrow racing boat; «togs» = clothes.
fact: Toad's crazes so far: «Once, it was nothing but sailing», then punting (pushing a flat boat along with a long pole), then «Last year it was house-boating». «he gets tired of it, and starts on something fresh.»
fact: The Otter: «Such a good fellow, too ... But no stability—especially in a boat!» — a joke: stability means both staying steady in your plans and not tipping over.
fact: Toad is «a short, stout figure—splashing badly and rolling a good deal, but working his hardest»; when the Rat calls to him he shakes his head and keeps on rowing.
fact: «A swirl of water and a "cloop!" and the May-fly was visible no more. Neither was the Otter.» — the Otter dived after the May-fly to eat it. Then «again there was a streak of bubbles on the surface of the river».
fact: «animal-etiquette forbade any sort of comment on the sudden disappearance of one's friends at any moment, for any reason or no reason whatever.»
fact: Packing up: a plate «staring up at him from the grass», a fork «which anybody ought to have seen», and «the mustard pot, which he had been sitting on without knowing it».
fact: «Packing the basket was not quite such pleasant work as unpacking the basket. It never is.»
fact: Old words: «provender» = food; «forthwith» = at once; «rumpus» = noise and fuss; «athwart» = across; «whereon» = on which; «Greedy beggars!» = a friendly tease, not a real insult.
fact: Talk in this lesson: «yeah nah» (Australia, NZ) = no; «craic» (Ireland) = fun, chat; «delulu» = believing something silly; «gadgie» (North East England) = bloke; «peng» (London) = great, tasty; «fixin' to» (Southern US) = about to.
exercise: What does the Mole notice moving along the water? | A streak of bubbles | A little blue-and-white boat | A May-fly | A floating mustard pot
exercise: Who makes the bubbles? | The Otter | A big fish called a pike | The Badger | Toad, rowing his racing boat
exercise: What are the Otter's first words? | «Greedy beggars! Why didn't you invite me, Ratty?» | «Hullo, Mole!» | «Proud, I'm sure» | «Look out, Toad's coming!»
exercise: «This was an impromptu affair». What does «impromptu» mean? | Not planned ahead | Very grand and expensive, with lots of guests | Secret | Very boring
exercise: What does the Otter say when he meets the Mole? | «Proud, I'm sure» | «Greedy beggars!» | «Go away, this is my backwater and you weren't asked» | «Who are you?»
exercise: What does the Otter say he came up the backwater to find? | A moment's peace | The Rat's lunch basket, which he could smell from the river | Toad's new racing boat | A May-fly
exercise: Whose «stripy head, with high shoulders» peeps out of the hedge? | Badger's | The Otter's | A weasel's, with hard eyes | Toad's
exercise: What does Badger say when he sees them? | «H'm! Company» | «Come on, old Badger!» | «Greedy beggars! Why wasn't I asked to lunch too?» | «Good day»
exercise: Why does Badger leave at once? | He hates Society | He is late for his dinner in the Wild Wood | He is scared of the Rat | The Otter was rude to him
exercise: What is Toad rowing? | A brand-new wager-boat | A house-boat | A little blue-and-white boat | A punt
exercise: «new togs, new everything!» What are «togs»? | Clothes | Long oars for racing boats | Ropes for tying up | Boats
exercise: What was Toad's craze before house-boating? | Punting | Motor-cars | Caravans | Swimming the length of the river
exercise: What happens to each of Toad's crazes? | He gets tired of it and starts something new | He sticks to it all his life and becomes very good at it | He gives it to the Rat | He sells it
exercise: How is Toad rowing when they see him? | Splashing badly and rolling, but trying his hardest | Smoothly and fast, like a champion | Slowly and lazily | Backwards
exercise: What does Toad do when the Rat hails him? | Shakes his head and rows on | Stops and comes over for lunch | Falls in | Waves both oars and shouts «Hooray!»
exercise: What interrupts the Otter's funny story about Toad? | A May-fly, which the Otter dives after | A shout from Toad, who has fallen into the river | Badger | Rain
exercise: What sound does the water make as the Otter goes? | «cloop!» | «Sploosh!» | «Poop-poop!» | «pop!»
exercise: Why doesn't the Rat say anything when the Otter vanishes? | Animal-etiquette forbids comment on it | He didn't notice it at all, as he was asleep | He's sad | He's angry
exercise: Who packs the basket after lunch? | The Mole | The Rat | The Otter | They both do it together
exercise: What was the Mole sitting on without knowing it? | The mustard pot | A fork that anybody ought to have seen | A plate | The Rat's straw hat
exercise: «Packing the basket was not quite such pleasant work as unpacking the basket. It never is.» Who says «It never is»? | The writer, to the reader | The Rat | The Otter | The Mole
exercise: «making for the provender». What is «provender»? | Food | The river | The boat | The bank
test: Kai, yeah nah (no) about it: «the Otter was dead rude and the Mole didn't like him.» Put it right. | They were friends «forthwith» | Right: they argued all afternoon and the Mole went home | The Otter never spoke | The Mole was rude
test: The Otter says he wanted «a moment's peace» and then «I beg pardon—I don't exactly mean that». Why does he apologise? | It sounded like he didn't want to see them | He was too late for lunch | He splashed them | He's late for Toad
test: The Otter says Toad has «no stability—especially in a boat». What's the double joke? | Toad keeps changing hobbies, and his boat rocks too | Toad has no stable for his horses or his ponies, as he sold them all for boats | Toad can't swim | Toad has too many boats
test: Ruby, bit delulu (believing something silly): «Toad sails, punts and house-boats every day, he loves all three, forever.» Put it right. | He does one at a time and gets bored | Right: he does all three every single day of the year | He hates boats | He only swims
test: Why do the Rat and the Otter laugh when they hear about Toad's new boat? | They know it's just his latest craze | Toad is a famous champion rower | The boat is pink | Toad fell in this morning
test: Badger says «H'm! Company» and leaves. What does the Rat say about him? | He «Simply hates Society!» | He loves parties and dancing and goes to every one on the river | He's the Rat's brother | He fears weasels
test: Did the Otter vanish because he was angry? | No: he dived after the May-fly | Yes: the Mole was very rude to him about the lunch | Yes: he hated the food | No: Badger called him
test: The Mole sees the bubbles again after the Otter vanishes. What does that tell him? | The Otter is swimming away underwater | It is going to rain very hard | A fish is drowning | The boat has a hole
test: What would a polite animal on the river bank say when a friend suddenly disappears? | Nothing at all | «Where did you go?» | «How rude!» | «Come back here at once and finish your story!»
test: «the Mole was bent on enjoying everything». What does «bent on» mean? | Determined to | Bent over | Too tired for | Unhappy about
test: Looking back: what did the Rat say about how crowded the bank is? | Too crowded — everyone wants you to do something | Empty and lonely, with only the river to talk to all year round | Full of weasels | Nobody lives there
test: Looking back: before lunch the Rat named Badger as living where? | In the heart of the Wild Wood | Next door to the Rat, in the same bank | In Toad Hall | Beyond the Wide World
test: Looking back: what did the Mole do at the backwater before lunch, gasping «O my! O my!» at each packet? | Unpacked the basket | Packed the basket up again, very slowly and carefully | Rowed | Swam
test: Looking back: the Mole hasn't been in a boat before today. Who else falls about in a boat in this chapter so far? | The Rat, when he crashed into the bank | The Otter, when he jumped in to eat | Badger | Nobody
test: ★★★ The Otter is «Proud, I'm sure» to meet the Mole, yet earlier said «Greedy beggars!» to them both. Was the Otter cross? | No: «Greedy beggars!» was a friendly tease | Yes: he was furious and then calmed down | Yes: he never forgave them | No: he was talking to the ducks
test: ★★★ Why is it funny that the Mole sat on the mustard pot? | He was hunting everywhere for packed things, and one was under him the whole time | Mustard pots are very hot to sit on | The pot was the Rat's favourite | It broke
test: ★★★ Which animals has the Mole now actually seen (not only heard about) in chapter I? | Rabbits, the Rat, the Otter, Badger and Toad | Weasels, stoats and foxes, from the boat | Only the Rat | The Rat, the Otter and a fox
exam: Identify the creature whose arrival was heralded by a streak of bubbles upon the surface of the water. | The Otter | The Badger | The Toad | A May-fly
exam: Ascertain the utterance with which the Badger acknowledged the presence of the party before withdrawing. | «H'm! Company» | «Proud, I'm sure» | «Greedy beggars!» | «Good day to you, gentlemen»
exam: Select the response which most accurately characterises Toad's conduct in respect of his pastimes. | He adopts each eagerly and soon tires of it | He has remained faithful to sailing throughout his entire life without exception | He has no pastimes | He pursues all at once
exam: It is given that the Otter vanished in pursuit of a May-fly. State the convention which governed the Rat's response. | Etiquette forbade comment on a friend's sudden disappearance | He was obliged to give chase | He must call for help | He must pack the basket
